Project name
[Mind Peace]

Purpose
Build a public mental health support tool that helps users with one clear need, such as mood check-ins, grounding exercises, journaling, or finding support resources. The app will support well-being, not replace therapy or emergency care.
who
+1

Target users
Students.

Young adults.

People who want simple, low-pressure mental health support.

Users on low-data or older devices.

Core principles
Keep the scope narrow.

Use plain language.

Protect privacy.

Include crisis support.

Design with lived-experience feedback.

Make the tool accessible and culturally relevant.


Milestones
M1: Discovery
Define the single problem the app solves.

Write the “what it does” and “what it does not do” statement.

Identify 5–10 people to interview for feedback.

Draft a simple feature list.

M2: Co-design
Interview users and a mental health professional.

Review feedback on language, trust, safety, and usefulness.

Decide the first release features.

Adjust the scope based on what people actually need.


M3: Prototype
Build a basic clickable or working prototype.

Add one main function.

Add a crisis/help screen.

Keep sign-up optional or minimal.


M4: Safety review
Write a short privacy policy.

Check what data is collected and why.

Add delete/export options.

Review crisis messaging and emergency contacts.


M5: User testing
Test with a small group.

Watch where users get confused.

Check accessibility, readability, and mobile usability.

Fix the biggest problems first.


M6: Pilot launch
Release to a limited public audience.

Collect feedback on usefulness, trust, and ease of use.

Monitor retention and any safety issues.

Make improvements before wider release.


M7: Public release
Publish version 1.

Share a clear explanation of who it is for.

State limitations and safety boundaries.

Keep updating based on user feedback.
